{
  "passage": "Changes around us can be grouped into two kinds. In a physical change, a substance changes its shape, size or state, but no new substance is formed. When ice melts into water, it is still water. When a sheet of paper is cut into pieces, each piece is still paper. Many physical changes can be reversed: water can be frozen back into ice. In a chemical change, one or more new substances are formed. When a piece of paper burns, it turns into ash, smoke and gases, and you cannot get the paper back. Burning of wood, cooking of food, rusting of iron and the souring of milk into curd are all chemical changes. A chemical change is often shown by signs such as the release of heat or light, a change in colour, the production of a gas, or a change in smell. When baking soda is added to vinegar, bubbles of carbon dioxide gas are produced, which shows that a new substance has formed. Some processes involve both kinds of change. When a candle burns, the wax first melts, which is a physical change. The wax vapour then burns in the flame to produce carbon dioxide, water vapour, heat and light, which is a chemical change. Crystallisation, in which pure crystals of a substance such as copper sulphate are obtained from its solution, is a physical change.",
